---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj19.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Completing the Staged Reform From Statements No. 3 and No. 14" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Consolidated Statements — Immediate Recognition Within Net Assets" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Individual (Non-Consolidated) Statements — Continued Deferred Recognition" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. THE KEY DIVERGENCE — Two Different Treatments Within the Same Company" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Recycling — From Net Assets to the Statement of Income" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Consolidated Recognition of an Actuarial Loss" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — the Same Loss Under Individual Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 19 Parallel" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    title="Status Note"
    explanation="Unlike Statements No. 3 and No. 14 (the earlier reform stages, both now abolished), this Part 3 amendment's substance remains integrated into today's ASBJ Statement No. 26 — it established the CURRENT, still-operative distinction between consolidated and individual (non-consolidated) retirement benefit accounting described below."
    historical="Extended reference poster. Self-contained, no internet connection required."
    link="/japangaap/asbj26"
    linkText="ASBJ Statement No. 26 — Accounting Standard for Retirement Benefits"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
<div class="section-header" id="s1">
  <span class="num">1</span>
  <h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">The final, culminating stage of Japan's staged retirement benefits reform</div>
    <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 19 completed the THIRD and FINAL stage of Japan's staged retirement benefits reform — following Statement No. 3 (Part 1) and Statement No. 14 (Part 2), both now ABOLISHED (see the companion posters). This FINAL stage established what remains, TODAY, one of the MOST genuinely DISTINCTIVE features of J-GAAP retirement benefit accounting: a DIFFERENT treatment of actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost depending on whether CONSOLIDATED or INDIVIDUAL financial statements are being prepared.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s2">
  <span class="num">2</span>
  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Companies sponsoring defined benefit retirement plans</div>
    <div class="principle">This Statement applies to companies sponsoring DEFINED BENEFIT retirement plans (including Japan's distinctively common lump-sum retirement allowance arrangements), addressing the recognition of ACTUARIAL gains/losses and PRIOR SERVICE COST specifically.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s3">
  <span class="num">3</span>
  <h2>Completing the Staged Reform From Statements No. 3 and No. 14</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">The final building block toward today's comprehensive standard</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Building on the EARLIER stages' progressive move toward FULLER Balance Sheet recognition (see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 3 and No. 14 posters), this THIRD stage introduced the SPECIFIC mechanism by which unrecognised actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost would be recognised MORE FULLY — but, DISTINCTIVELY, it did so DIFFERENTLY depending on whether the reporting entity was preparing CONSOLIDATED or INDIVIDUAL financial statements, a nuance that REMAINS a live feature of TODAY's ASBJ Statement No. 26.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s4">
  <span class="num">4</span>
  <h2>Consolidated Statements — Immediate Recognition Within Net Assets</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">A "Remeasurements of Defined Benefit Plans" component, closely mirroring post-2011 IAS 19</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">In CONSOLIDATED financial statements, actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost ARISING during the period are recognised IMMEDIATELY within Net Assets — specifically within a DEDICATED "Remeasurements of Defined Benefit Plans" line WITHIN the Valuation and Translation Adjustments component (see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 5 poster) — RATHER than being deferred and gradually amortised through profit or loss. This closely MIRRORS the IASB's OWN post-2011 amendments to IAS 19, which eliminated the "corridor approach" and required immediate OCI recognition of remeasurements.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s5">
  <span class="num">5</span>
  <h2>Individual (Non-Consolidated) Statements — Continued Deferred Recognition</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">A more gradual, delayed recognition pattern persists</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">By CONTRAST, in an entity's OWN INDIVIDUAL (non-consolidated, parent-only) financial statements, companies CONTINUE to apply a MORE GRADUAL, DELAYED recognition pattern for actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost — amortising these amounts through profit or loss over a period (often tied to employees' average remaining service period) RATHER than recognising them immediately within Net Assets, as consolidated reporting REQUIRES.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s6">
  <span class="num">6</span>
  <h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — Two Different Treatments Within the Same Company</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely distinctive, still-current split by statement type</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">This is a GENUINELY DISTINCTIVE, well-recognised, and STILL-CURRENT feature of J-GAAP: the SAME underlying actuarial gain or loss can be treated DIFFERENTLY depending SOLELY on WHICH set of financial statements is being prepared — IMMEDIATE recognition within Net Assets for CONSOLIDATED purposes, but DEFERRED, gradual recognition through profit or loss for INDIVIDUAL (parent-only) statutory purposes. This is UNLIKE most other frameworks discussed in this poster series, where a SINGLE accounting policy generally applies REGARDLESS of whether consolidated or standalone statements are being prepared — J-GAAP's retirement benefits standard is a NOTABLE exception to that general pattern.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s7">
  <span class="num">7</span>
  <h2>Recycling — From Net Assets to the Statement of Income</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Consolidated remeasurements are eventually reclassified through profit or loss</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Amounts recognised WITHIN the "Remeasurements of Defined Benefit Plans" Net Assets component (for consolidated purposes) are NOT left there permanently — they are subsequently RECLASSIFIED ("recycled") to the Statement of Income OVER the employees' AVERAGE remaining service period, broadly PARALLELING how the amount would EVENTUALLY be recognised under the INDIVIDUAL statement treatment, just on a DIFFERENT initial recognition timeline.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s8">
  <span class="num">8</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — Consolidated Recognition of an Actuarial Loss</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Immediate recognition within Net Assets</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: A ¥60,000,000 actuarial loss arising during the year</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A company's defined benefit plan experiences a ¥60,000,000 actuarial LOSS during the year, due to a change in discount rate assumptions.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">In the company's CONSOLIDATED financial statements, the FULL ¥60,000,000 loss is recognised<strong>IMMEDIATELY</strong>within the "Remeasurements of Defined Benefit Plans" component of Net Assets — NOT deferred or gradually amortised — though it will subsequently be RECYCLED to the Statement of Income over the employees' average remaining service period.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s9">
  <span class="num">9</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — the Same Loss Under Individual Financial Statements</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">A different, more gradual treatment for the same underlying loss</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: The same ¥60,000,000 loss, individual (parent-only) statements</div>
      <div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, assume the average remaining service period of plan participants is 10 years.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">In the company's OWN INDIVIDUAL (non-consolidated) financial statements, the SAME ¥60,000,000 loss is instead AMORTISED GRADUALLY — for example, ¥6,000,000 per year over the 10-year average remaining service period — RATHER than recognised immediately, illustrating the GENUINELY DIFFERENT treatment the SAME underlying event receives DEPENDING SOLELY on which set of statements is being prepared.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s10">
  <span class="num">10</span>
  <h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">What this Statement's substance (within ASBJ Statement No. 26) requires</div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>Movement in the retirement benefit obligation, plan assets, and net liability/asset recognised</span><span>Amounts and timing basis for recognition of actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost, distinguishing consolidated from individual treatment</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s11">
  <span class="num">11</span>
  <h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Determining the appropriate amortisation period for individual (non-consolidated) statement purposes</span></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Actuarial assumptions (discount rate, expected return on plan assets, mortality, turnover) underlying the retirement benefit obligation</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s12">
  <span class="num">12</span>
  <h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 19 Parallel</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IAS 19</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP consolidated / individual</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 19, post-2011)</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Consolidated treatment</span><span>Immediate recognition within Net Assets</span><span>Immediate recognition within OCI — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Individual/separate statement treatment</span><span>Continued GRADUAL, deferred amortisation</span><span>IAS 19 does NOT distinguish separate from consolidated statements — the SAME immediate-recognition treatment applies throughout</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">J-GAAP's CONSOLIDATED treatment is genuinely CONVERGED with modern IAS 19 — but the CONTINUED existence of a SEPARATE, more gradual treatment for INDIVIDUAL statements is a DISTINCTIVELY J-GAAP feature, without an equivalent DUAL-TRACK structure under IFRS.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s13">
  <span class="num">13</span>
  <h2>Statement Mapping</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Where every retirement benefit remeasurement lands</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Actuarial gain/loss (consolidated)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span></span><span>Immediate recognition, later recycled to profit or loss</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Actuarial gain/loss (individual)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Gradual amortisation over the average remaining service period</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s14">
  <span class="num">14</span>
  <h2>Related Standards</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 26</span><span>The comprehensive current standard this Statement's substance is fully integrated into</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 5</span><span>Supplies the Valuation and Translation Adjustments Net Assets component consolidated remeasurements are recognised within</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s15">
  <span class="num">15</span>
  <h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item">
      <div class="glossary-term">Remeasurements of Defined Benefit Plans</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">The consolidated Net Assets component within Valuation and Translation Adjustments capturing actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost as they arise.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
      <div class="glossary-term">Average remaining service period</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">The period over which unrecognised actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost are amortised in individual (non-consolidated) financial statements.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="legend">
  <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
  <div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span>Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)<span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span>Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)<span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span>純資産の部<span class="badge be">SCNA</span>Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)<span class="badge bc">SCF</span>Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div>
</div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
